<decompilertest>
<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:32:default:gcc">
<!--
    RE-friction round 10, challenge 64f1f7fad931496abf909535 (i386 Windows PE
    `illusion.exe`): `kuna decompile ... 0x401571` opened the recovered
    key-derivation helper with a re-declaration of its own first parameter,

        void sub_401571(unsigned int a0,int4 a1,int4 a2)
        {
          int4 a0;
          ...
          v1 = CryptDeriveKey(v4,&amp;a0,4,0);

    where the `&amp;a0` line maps to `LEA ECX,[EBP + 0x8]`, the incoming parameter's
    own slot on an i386 cdecl frame.  Two objects of one name and two types in
    one scope is not valid C, so a `decompile-project` export of the function
    cannot be recompiled.

    Root cause (P9, naming policy).  Upstream declares a body's locals by walking
    the ScopeLocal Symbol table for `no_category` Symbols only
    (PrintC::emitScopeVarDecls, printc.cc:2667), so a `function_parameter`
    Symbol renders in the signature and nowhere else.  kuna's printer walks
    HighVariables instead and asks whether a `function_parameter` Symbol
    CONTAINS THE STORAGE of one of the high's member Varnodes.  An address-taken
    parameter has no such member: ActionNameVars::linkSpacebaseSymbol
    (coreaction.cc:3005) encodes `&amp;symbol` as the offset CONSTANT of a
    PTRSUB(spacebase, off) and binds that constant's high to the Symbol owning
    the slot, so when the body never touches the slot any other way every
    Varnode of the high lives in the constant space.  The storage test answers
    "not a parameter", and the high is declared in the body under the name it
    took from the parameter Symbol it is bound to.

    Stage / sub-stage: P9 "naming-policy".  The P0 assertion
    `option paramrefdecl on|off` decides whether the printer may use the Symbol
    IDENTITY the reference already carries (HighVariable::kuna_ref_symbol, the
    port of Varnode::setSymbolReference) instead of the storage containment
    test.  A reference to a `no_category` local is a different category and is
    untouched.

    The fixture is the same construct, hand assembled: `derive_key` is an i386
    cdecl function that passes its first parameter BY ADDRESS
    (`lea 0x8(%ebp),%eax`) and never reads that slot as a value, so `&amp;a0` is the
    parameter's only appearance.

    Pass 1 (`option paramrefdecl off`) reproduces the BUG: the parameter is
    declared a second time in the body.
    Pass 2 (`option paramrefdecl on`) is the FIX: the body declares nothing and
    the `&amp;a0` argument resolves to the signature's own parameter.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000">
5589e58d4508ff750c50e80500000083c4085dc38b4424048b0003442408c3
0000000000000000
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="derive_key"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100014" name="consume"/>
</binaryimage>
<script>
  <com>option paramrefdecl off</com>
  <com>lo fu derive_key</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>option paramrefdecl on</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>quit</com>
</script>
<stringmatch name="paramrefdecl #1: option off re-declares the signature parameter as a body local (bug reproduced)" min="1" max="1">^ *[a-z0-9_]+ a0;$</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="paramrefdecl #2: the address-taken parameter is passed by address in both passes" min="2" max="2">consume\(&amp;a0,a1\);</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="paramrefdecl #3: the parameter keeps its place in the signature in both passes" min="2" max="2">^void derive_key\(unsigned int a0,unsigned int a1\)$</stringmatch>
</decompilertest>
